1. Custom Resources & Operators
    - Kubernetes is extensible through Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and Operators, allowing you to manage complex applications using Kubernetes-native APIs.

2. Custom Resources (CRDs)
    - A Custom Resource Definition (CRD) lets you create your own Kubernetes object types.
    - Kubernetes already includes built-in resources such as:
        Pods
        Deployments
        Services
    - CRDs allow you to define resources like:
        Database
        Backup
        CacheCluster
        MessageQueue

3. Why Use CRDs?
    - CRDs are useful when:
        Managing complex applications
        Automating operational tasks
        Extending Kubernetes APIs
        Creating platform-specific abstractions
    - Example:
    Instead of manually configuring a database:
        apiVersion: databases.example.com/v1
        kind: Database
        metadata:
            name: production-db
        spec:
            engine: postgres
            version: "16"
            storage: 100Gi
    - A controller/operator can automatically:
        Create StatefulSets
        Configure backups
        Handle failover
        Manage upgrades
    - CRD Architecture
    Components
        1. Custom Resource Definition
            Defines schema and API structure
        2. Custom Resource (CR)
            Instance of the custom object
        3. Controller / Operator
            Watches resources and reconciles desired state

4. Creating a CRD
    - Example CRD
        apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
        kind: CustomResourceDefinition
        metadata:
            name: databases.example.com
        spec:
            group: example.com
        names:
            plural: databases
            singular: database
            kind: Database
            shortNames:
                - db
        scope: Namespaced
        versions:
            - name: v1
            served: true
            storage: true
            schema:
                openAPIV3Schema:
                    type: object
                    properties:
                        spec:
                            type: object
                            properties:
                                engine:
                                    type: string
                                version:
                                    type: string
                                storage:
                                    type: string

5. Creating a Custom Resource
    apiVersion: example.com/v1
    kind: Database
    metadata:
        name: app-db
    spec:
        engine: postgres
        version: "16"
        storage: 50Gi

6. Operators
    - An Operator is a Kubernetes controller that automates application management using CRDs.
    - Operators follow the control loop pattern:
        1. Watch resources
        2. Compare desired vs actual state
        3. Reconcile differences automatically

7. What Operators Can Do
    - Operators automate:
        Deployment
        Scaling
        Failover
        Backups
        Recovery
        Upgrades
        Certificate rotation
    - Examples:
        Database clustering
        Kafka management
        Monitoring stacks

8. Operator Workflow
    Custom Resource
        ↓
    Operator watches events
        ↓
    Controller reconciliation loop
        ↓
    Creates/updates Kubernetes resources
        ↓
    Desired state maintained

